  "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\u22ef,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+\u22ef\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=10$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{10}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & \u22483+\\frac{1}{6}(10-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(10-9)^{2} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{216} \\\\  & =3.162\\,04,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places.",
